window.LB = window.LB || {};
LB["p5-respcirc"] = {
  notes: [
    { h: "The respiratory system", t: "The human respiratory system is made up of the nose, the windpipe and the lungs. Its function is to take in oxygen from the air and to remove carbon dioxide from the body. We have two lungs, found in the chest.", kw: ["nose, windpipe and lungs", "takes in oxygen", "removes carbon dioxide"] },
    { h: "The path of air", t: "When we breathe in, air enters through the nose, passes down the windpipe and goes into the lungs. The windpipe carries air to and from the lungs. When we breathe out, air leaves the lungs through the windpipe and out of the nose. Our chest rises when we breathe in and falls when we breathe out.", kw: ["nose → windpipe → lungs", "windpipe carries air to and from the lungs"] },
    { h: "Gaseous exchange in the lungs", t: "Gaseous exchange takes place only in the lungs, which have many tiny air sacs surrounded by blood vessels. Oxygen from the air in the lungs enters the blood. Carbon dioxide from the blood passes into the lungs and is removed from the body when we breathe out.", kw: ["gaseous exchange takes place in the lungs", "oxygen enters the blood", "carbon dioxide is removed"] },
    { h: "Inhaled air vs exhaled air", t: "Both inhaled air and exhaled air are mixtures of gases, and both are mostly nitrogen (about 78%). Inhaled air has more oxygen (about 21%) than exhaled air (about 16%). Exhaled air has more carbon dioxide (about 4% compared with about 0.04%) and more water vapour. The amount of nitrogen stays about the same because the body does not use nitrogen.", kw: ["more oxygen in inhaled air", "more carbon dioxide and water vapour in exhaled air", "nitrogen is about the same"] },
    { h: "Breathing rate and exercise", t: "Breathing rate is the number of breaths taken in one minute. During exercise, the muscles need more energy, so the body needs more oxygen and produces more carbon dioxide. We breathe faster and deeper to take in more oxygen and remove more carbon dioxide at a faster rate. After exercise, the breathing rate slowly returns to the resting rate.", kw: ["breathing rate increases", "take in more oxygen", "remove more carbon dioxide"] },
    { h: "How fish take in oxygen", t: "Fish take in oxygen through their gills. Water enters the mouth and passes over the gills, which take in the oxygen dissolved in the water; the water then leaves through the gill openings. Gills can only take in oxygen dissolved in water, so a fish taken out of water cannot take in enough oxygen and dies.", kw: ["gills", "oxygen dissolved in water"] },
    { h: "How insects take in air", t: "Insects take in air through tiny breathing holes (spiracles) along the sides of their bodies. The breathing holes lead to air tubes that carry air to all parts of the insect’s body. If the breathing holes are blocked, for example by oil, the insect cannot take in air and dies.", kw: ["breathing holes", "spiracles", "air tubes"] },
    { h: "How plants take in air", t: "Plants need oxygen too. They take in air through tiny openings (pores) in their leaves, and most of these openings are on the lower surface of the leaf. Gases enter and leave the leaf through these tiny openings. Different living things use different parts, but all of them take in the oxygen they need.", kw: ["tiny openings in the leaves", "pores", "lower surface"] },
    { h: "The circulatory system", t: "The circulatory system is made up of the heart, the blood and the blood vessels. The heart is a muscular organ that pumps blood around the body. Blood vessels carry blood from the heart to all parts of the body and back to the heart.", kw: ["heart pumps blood", "blood vessels carry blood", "to all parts of the body"] },
    { h: "What blood transports", t: "Blood transports oxygen, digested food and water to all parts of the body. It also carries carbon dioxide and other waste substances away from the body parts. Blood carries carbon dioxide to the lungs, where it is removed from the body. Blood leaving the lungs has more oxygen and less carbon dioxide than blood entering the lungs.", kw: ["oxygen, digested food and water", "carbon dioxide and waste", "to the lungs"] },
    { h: "Heart rate and pulse", t: "Heart rate is the number of heartbeats in one minute. Each heartbeat pushes blood through the blood vessels, which we can feel as a pulse at the wrist or neck, so the pulse rate is the same as the heart rate. During exercise, the heart beats faster to pump blood faster, so that more oxygen and digested food are transported to the muscles and more carbon dioxide is removed. To get beats per minute from a 15-second count, multiply by 4.", kw: ["heart beats faster", "pulse", "pump blood faster"] },
    { h: "Systems working together", t: "The digestive system breaks down food into simpler substances, which are absorbed into the blood in the small intestine. The respiratory system takes in oxygen, which enters the blood in the lungs. The circulatory system transports the digested food and oxygen to all parts of the body, where they are used to release energy, and carries carbon dioxide back to the lungs to be removed.", kw: ["absorbed into the blood", "enters the blood in the lungs", "transports digested food and oxygen"] }
  ],
  traps: [
    "“We breathe in oxygen and breathe out carbon dioxide” is incomplete -> both inhaled and exhaled air are mixtures; exhaled air still has about 16% oxygen and is mostly nitrogen.",
    "Exhaled air has more nitrogen -> the amount of nitrogen is about the same, because the body does not use nitrogen.",
    "Gaseous exchange happens in the nose or windpipe -> it happens ONLY in the lungs; the nose and windpipe just carry air.",
    "Fish breathe in air through gills or split water to get oxygen -> gills take in the oxygen DISSOLVED in water.",
    "Dolphins and whales have gills -> they have lungs and must come up to the water surface to take in air.",
    "Insects breathe through their mouths -> they take in air through breathing holes (spiracles) along the sides of their bodies.",
    "The heart pumps oxygen / the heart makes blood -> the heart pumps BLOOD; the blood carries the oxygen.",
    "The heart beats faster during exercise so that the heart gets more oxygen -> it beats faster to transport more oxygen and digested food to the MUSCLES and remove more carbon dioxide."
  ],
  lessons: [
    {
      h: "Explaining faster breathing and heart rate during exercise",
      concept: "Markers want the full chain, not just ‘she is tired’. During exercise the muscles need more energy. To release that energy they need more oxygen and digested food, and they produce more carbon dioxide. Faster breathing takes in more oxygen and removes more carbon dioxide at a faster rate; a faster heart rate pumps blood faster, so more oxygen and digested food are transported to the muscles and carbon dioxide is carried away more quickly.",
      example: {
        q: "The graph shows Jia Hui’s heart rate before, during and after skipping. (a) What was her heart rate at rest? (b) Explain why her heart rate increased when she skipped.",
        fig: { type: "line", title: "Jia Hui’s heart rate", xl: "Time (min)", yl: "Heart rate (beats per min)", pts: [["0", 72], ["2", 72], ["4", 118], ["6", 128], ["8", 95], ["10", 76]] },
        marks: 3,
        think: [
          "Step 1: Read the flat part at the start of the graph: 72 beats per minute before she started.",
          "Step 2: Link skipping to the muscles: they need more energy, so they need more oxygen and digested food.",
          "Step 3: Say what the heart does: it beats faster to pump blood faster.",
          "Step 4: Say what the blood does: it transports more oxygen and digested food to the muscles more quickly (and removes more carbon dioxide)."
        ],
        answer: "(a) 72 beats per minute. (b) Her muscles needed more energy when she skipped. Her heart beat faster to pump blood faster, so that more oxygen and digested food could be transported to her muscles more quickly, and more carbon dioxide could be removed."
      },
      tip: "Breathing rate = taking in oxygen and removing carbon dioxide. Heart rate = transporting oxygen and digested food. Do not mix up the two organs’ jobs.",
      try: {
        q: "The bar graph shows Mei’s breathing rate during three activities. Which statement best explains why her breathing rate was highest when she was running?",
        fig: { type: "bar", xl: "Activity", yl: "Breathing rate (breaths per min)", bars: [["Sitting", 16], ["Walking", 24], ["Running", 42]] },
        o: ["Her lungs grew bigger while running, so they could hold and move more air each breath.", "Her body needed to breathe out more nitrogen, which her muscles made while running.", "Her muscles needed more energy, so she took in more oxygen and removed more carbon dioxide.", "Her heart had stopped pumping, so her lungs had to work harder to move blood."],
        a: 2,
        why: "Running needs the most energy, so the body needs the most oxygen and produces the most carbon dioxide. The lungs do not grow, nitrogen is not used by the body, and the heart pumps faster, not stops."
      }
    },
    {
      h: "Reading inhaled vs exhaled air data",
      concept: "Questions often hide the gases as letters. Use the patterns: the gas that stays the same (about 78%) is nitrogen, because the body does not use it. The gas that DROPS from about 21% to about 16% is oxygen, because it enters the blood in the lungs. The gas that RISES from about 0.04% to about 4% is carbon dioxide, because it is removed from the blood. Exhaled air also has more water vapour.",
      example: {
        q: "The table shows the percentage of three gases, X, Y and Z, in inhaled and exhaled air. (a) Identify gases Y and Z. (b) Explain why the amount of gas X is the same in inhaled and exhaled air.",
        tbl: [["Gas", "Inhaled air (%)", "Exhaled air (%)"], ["X", "78", "78"], ["Y", "21", "16"], ["Z", "0.04", "4"]],
        marks: 3,
        think: [
          "Step 1: Y decreases after breathing, so the body took some in: Y is oxygen.",
          "Step 2: Z increases, so the body gave some out: Z is carbon dioxide.",
          "Step 3: X does not change and is the biggest part of air: X is nitrogen, which the body does not use."
        ],
        answer: "(a) Y is oxygen and Z is carbon dioxide. (b) X is nitrogen. The body does not use nitrogen, so the same amount is breathed out as is breathed in."
      },
      tip: "Exhaled air is NOT ‘only carbon dioxide’. Even exhaled air has far more oxygen (about 16%) than carbon dioxide (about 4%).",
      try: {
        q: "The bar graph shows the percentage of gas G in inhaled air and in exhaled air. What is gas G?",
        fig: { type: "bar", xl: "Type of air", yl: "Amount of gas G (%)", bars: [["Inhaled air", 21], ["Exhaled air", 16]] },
        o: ["Nitrogen", "Carbon dioxide", "Water vapour", "Oxygen"],
        a: 3,
        why: "Gas G falls from about 21% to about 16% because some of it enters the blood in the lungs, so G is oxygen. Nitrogen stays about 78%, while carbon dioxide and water vapour increase in exhaled air."
      }
    },
    {
      h: "Tracing gases through the body",
      concept: "Oxygen travels: nose → windpipe → lungs → (enters the blood) → heart → blood vessels → body parts. Carbon dioxide travels the other way: body parts → blood vessels → heart → lungs → (removed when we breathe out) → windpipe → nose. The key sentence for the lungs is: oxygen from the air in the lungs enters the blood, and carbon dioxide from the blood passes into the lungs to be removed.",
      example: {
        q: "The diagram shows the human respiratory system. (a) Name part C. (b) State what happens to oxygen in part C. (c) State what happens to carbon dioxide in part C.",
        fig: { type: "organs", system: "respiratory", mark: { nose: "A", windpipe: "B", lungs: "C" } },
        marks: 3,
        think: [
          "Step 1: Follow the air path: A is where air enters (nose), B is the tube (windpipe), C is where the tube ends (lungs).",
          "Step 2: In the lungs, oxygen moves from the air into the blood.",
          "Step 3: Carbon dioxide moves from the blood into the lungs and is breathed out."
        ],
        answer: "(a) Lungs. (b) Oxygen from the air in the lungs enters the blood. (c) Carbon dioxide from the blood passes into the lungs and is removed from the body when we breathe out."
      },
      tip: "Always say where the gas goes FROM and TO (‘from the air in the lungs into the blood’). ‘Oxygen goes to the lungs’ scores nothing for gaseous exchange.",
      try: {
        q: "Which shows the correct path taken by carbon dioxide from the leg muscles until it leaves the body?",
        o: ["Leg muscles → lungs → heart → windpipe → nose", "Leg muscles → heart → windpipe → lungs → nose", "Leg muscles → windpipe → lungs → heart → nose", "Leg muscles → heart → lungs → windpipe → nose"],
        a: 3,
        why: "Blood carries carbon dioxide from the leg muscles back to the heart, which pumps it to the lungs. There it passes into the lungs and is breathed out through the windpipe and nose."
      }
    },
    {
      h: "How other organisms take in air: ‘why did it die?’ questions",
      concept: "Name the correct part for each organism: fish use gills (oxygen dissolved in water), insects use breathing holes (spiracles) connected to air tubes, plants use tiny openings (pores) in their leaves, and mammals such as dolphins use lungs. For ‘why did it die?’ questions, give the chain: the part is blocked or unsuitable → it cannot take in air/oxygen → it dies.",
      example: {
        q: "Oil was painted along both sides of a grasshopper’s body. The grasshopper died soon after. Explain why.",
        marks: 2,
        think: [
          "Step 1: Name the part: grasshoppers take in air through breathing holes along the sides of the body.",
          "Step 2: Say what the oil did: it blocked the breathing holes.",
          "Step 3: Give the result: no air (oxygen) could enter, so it died."
        ],
        answer: "The oil blocked the breathing holes along the sides of the grasshopper’s body, so it could not take in air (oxygen) and died."
      },
      tip: "Never write ‘it could not breathe’ on its own – always name the part (breathing holes, gills, tiny openings) and say what it could not take in.",
      try: {
        q: "A goldfish dies if it is kept out of water for too long, even though air contains plenty of oxygen. Which is the best explanation?",
        o: ["Its gills can only take in oxygen that is dissolved in water.", "It needs to drink water all the time to stay alive.", "Its lungs are too small to take in enough oxygen from the air.", "Its breathing holes dry up and close when out of water."],
        a: 0,
        why: "Fish take in oxygen dissolved in water through their gills. Goldfish have no lungs and no breathing holes; that is why they cannot use the oxygen in air."
      }
    },
    {
      h: "Linking the three systems",
      concept: "Many Booklet B questions ask how systems ‘work together’. Give one job per system and join them: the digestive system breaks down food into simpler substances that are absorbed into the blood in the small intestine; the respiratory system takes in oxygen, which enters the blood in the lungs; the circulatory system (the heart pumping blood through blood vessels) transports digested food and oxygen to all body parts and carries carbon dioxide back to the lungs.",
      example: {
        q: "The diagram shows the human digestive system. (a) Name part Q, where digested food enters the blood. (b) Explain how the digested food and oxygen reach the muscles of the leg.",
        fig: { type: "organs", system: "digestive", mark: { stomach: "P", small_intestine: "Q", large_intestine: "R" } },
        marks: 3,
        think: [
          "Step 1: Digested food is absorbed into the blood in the small intestine, so Q is the small intestine.",
          "Step 2: Oxygen enters the blood in the lungs.",
          "Step 3: The heart pumps the blood, and the blood vessels carry the digested food and oxygen to the leg muscles."
        ],
        answer: "(a) Small intestine. (b) Oxygen enters the blood in the lungs. The heart pumps the blood, which carries the digested food and oxygen through the blood vessels to the leg muscles."
      },
      tip: "The heart pumps; the blood carries; the blood vessels are the ‘roads’. Use each word for its own job.",
      try: {
        q: "Which of these statements are correct? A: Blood transports digested food absorbed in the small intestine. B: Oxygen enters the blood in the lungs. C: The heart breaks down food into simpler substances. D: Blood carries carbon dioxide from the body parts to the lungs.",
        o: ["A and B only", "B and D only", "A, B and D only", "A, B, C and D"],
        a: 2,
        why: "A, B and D are correct. C is wrong: digestion happens in the digestive system; the heart only pumps blood."
      }
    }
  ],
  mcq: [
    { q: "The diagram shows the human respiratory system. In which labelled part does oxygen enter the blood?", fig: { type: "organs", system: "respiratory", mark: { nose: "A", windpipe: "B", lungs: "C" } }, o: ["A", "B", "C", "A and B"], a: 2, why: "Gaseous exchange takes place only in the lungs (C). The nose (A) and windpipe (B) only carry air.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "The diagram shows the human circulatory system. What is part X and what does it do?", fig: { type: "organs", system: "circulatory", mark: { heart: "X" } }, o: ["Lung – takes in air", "Heart – pumps blood around the body", "Stomach – digests food", "Windpipe – carries air to the lungs"], a: 1, why: "X is the heart, a muscular organ that pumps blood around the body through the blood vessels.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "Which part does a fish use to take in oxygen?", o: ["Lungs", "Breathing holes", "Tiny openings in its scales", "Gills"], a: 3, why: "Fish take in oxygen dissolved in water through their gills. Breathing holes are found in insects.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "How does a grasshopper take in air?", o: ["Through breathing holes on its body", "Through gills under its wings", "Through a pair of lungs", "Through its nose and mouth"], a: 0, why: "Insects take in air through breathing holes (spiracles) along the sides of their bodies, which lead to air tubes.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "Plants take in air mainly through ______.", o: ["their colourful petals", "the pollen grains in flowers", "tiny openings in their leaves", "their fruits and seeds"], a: 2, why: "Leaves have tiny openings (pores), mostly on the lower surface, through which air enters and leaves.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "What is the function of the windpipe?", o: ["It carries air to and from the lungs.", "It pumps blood to the lungs.", "It is where oxygen enters the blood.", "It carries food to the stomach."], a: 0, why: "The windpipe carries air between the nose and the lungs. Oxygen enters the blood in the lungs, not the windpipe; the gullet carries food.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "Where on the body can Ali most easily feel his pulse?", o: ["Knee cap", "Fingernail", "Elbow tip", "Wrist"], a: 3, why: "The pulse can be felt at the wrist (or the side of the neck), where a blood vessel lies close to the skin.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "Which system is made up of the heart, blood and blood vessels?", o: ["Respiratory system", "Digestive system", "Circulatory system", "Skeletal system"], a: 2, why: "The heart, blood and blood vessels make up the circulatory system, which transports substances around the body.", lvl: 1 },

    { q: "The bar graph shows Mei’s breathing rate during three activities. Why was her breathing rate highest when she was running?", fig: { type: "bar", xl: "Activity", yl: "Breathing rate (breaths per min)", bars: [["Sitting", 15], ["Walking", 22], ["Running", 40]] }, o: ["Her lungs grow bigger when she runs, so they hold more air.", "She needs to breathe out more nitrogen made by her muscles.", "Her body needs more oxygen and must remove more carbon dioxide.", "Her heart stops pumping blood when she runs, so her lungs work harder."], a: 2, why: "Running muscles need more energy, so more oxygen must be taken in and more carbon dioxide removed. The body does not use or remove extra nitrogen.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "The diagram shows the human respiratory system. Which shows the path of air when we breathe in?", fig: { type: "organs", system: "respiratory", mark: { lungs: "P", nose: "Q", windpipe: "R" } }, o: ["R → Q → P", "Q → R → P", "Q → P → R", "P → R → Q"], a: 1, why: "Air enters through the nose (Q), passes down the windpipe (R) and goes into the lungs (P).", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Which of the following is NOT transported by the blood?", o: ["Oxygen", "Water", "Carbon dioxide", "Undigested food"], a: 3, why: "Blood transports oxygen, digested food, water and carbon dioxide. Undigested food stays in the gut and passes out through the anus.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Study the Venn diagram. Which animal could be P?", fig: { type: "venn", a: "Has lungs", b: "Lives in water", onlyA: ["cat"], both: ["P"], onlyB: ["goldfish"], neither: ["grasshopper"] }, o: ["Shark", "Stingray", "Guppy", "Dolphin"], a: 3, why: "A dolphin lives in water but has lungs, so it must come to the surface to take in air. Sharks, stingrays and guppies are fish that use gills.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Limewater turns chalky when carbon dioxide is passed through it. Ravi set up A and B as shown, using the same amount of limewater and the same volume of air. What would he most likely observe?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "A", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Limewater", "Exhaled air blown in"] }, { label: "B", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Limewater", "Surrounding air", "pumped in"] }] }, o: ["The limewater in A turns chalky faster than in B.", "The limewater in B turns chalky faster than in A.", "Both turn chalky at the same time.", "Neither turns chalky, as air has no carbon dioxide."], a: 0, why: "Exhaled air has much more carbon dioxide (about 4%) than surrounding air (about 0.04%), because carbon dioxide from the blood is removed in the lungs.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Oil was painted along the sides of a grasshopper’s body. It died soon after. Why?", o: ["The oil dried out its gills, so it could not take in air.", "Its wings could not move, so it could not escape or feed.", "The oil blocked its breathing holes, so no air could enter.", "The oil made its body too heavy for its legs to move."], a: 2, why: "Grasshoppers take in air through breathing holes along the sides of the body; blocking them stops air entering. Insects do not have gills.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "The diagram shows the circulatory system. Which labelled part carries blood to and from all parts of the body?", fig: { type: "organs", system: "circulatory", mark: { heart: "A", lungs: "B", blood_vessels: "C" } }, o: ["A", "B", "C", "A and B only"], a: 2, why: "The blood vessels (C) carry blood to and from all body parts. The heart (A) pumps the blood; the lungs (B) are where gaseous exchange happens.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "The graph shows Jia Hui’s heart rate over 10 minutes. During which period was she most likely skipping?", fig: { type: "line", xl: "Time (min)", yl: "Heart rate (beats per min)", pts: [["0", 72], ["2", 73], ["4", 120], ["6", 130], ["8", 96], ["10", 76]] }, o: ["0 to 2 min", "2 to 6 min", "6 to 10 min", "0 to 10 min"], a: 1, why: "Her heart rate rose sharply from 2 min and was highest at 6 min, then fell as she rested. Before 2 min it stayed at the resting rate.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "The table shows the percentage of three gases in inhaled and exhaled air. What is gas Y?", tbl: [["Gas", "Inhaled air (%)", "Exhaled air (%)"], ["X", "78", "78"], ["Y", "21", "16"], ["Z", "0.04", "4"]], o: ["Nitrogen", "Carbon dioxide", "Water vapour", "Oxygen"], a: 3, why: "Y drops from 21% to 16% because some of it enters the blood in the lungs, so it is oxygen. X (unchanged) is nitrogen; Z (increases) is carbon dioxide.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "The bar graph shows the percentage of gas G in inhaled and exhaled air. What is gas G?", fig: { type: "bar", xl: "Type of air", yl: "Amount of gas G (%)", bars: [["Inhaled air", 0.04], ["Exhaled air", 4]] }, o: ["Oxygen", "Carbon dioxide", "Nitrogen", "Hydrogen"], a: 1, why: "G increases greatly in exhaled air because carbon dioxide is removed from the blood in the lungs. Oxygen decreases and nitrogen stays the same.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "The diagram shows the human digestive system. In which part is digested food absorbed into the blood?", fig: { type: "organs", system: "digestive", mark: { stomach: "P", small_intestine: "Q", large_intestine: "R", gullet: "S" } }, o: ["P", "Q", "R", "S"], a: 1, why: "Digested food is absorbed into the blood in the small intestine (Q). The large intestine (R) mainly absorbs water.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Which statement about exhaled air is correct?", o: ["It contains no oxygen.", "It is made up mostly of carbon dioxide.", "It contains less nitrogen than inhaled air.", "It contains more water vapour than inhaled air."], a: 3, why: "Exhaled air has more water vapour. It is still mostly nitrogen (same amount as inhaled air) and still has about 16% oxygen.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Study the classification chart. Which correctly identifies A, B, C and D?", fig: { type: "flow", root: "Living things", node: { q: "Takes in air via leaves?", yes: "A", no: { q: "Has gills?", yes: "B", no: { q: "Has breathing holes?", yes: "C", no: "D" } } } }, o: ["A: fern, B: guppy, C: beetle, D: rabbit", "A: fern, B: dolphin, C: beetle, D: rabbit", "A: guppy, B: fern, C: beetle, D: rabbit", "A: fern, B: guppy, C: rabbit, D: beetle"], a: 0, why: "Ferns take in air through tiny openings in leaves, guppies use gills, beetles use breathing holes, and rabbits (lungs) have none of these. Dolphins have lungs, not gills.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "What causes the pulse that we feel at the wrist?", o: ["Air moving along the windpipe to the lungs", "The heart pumping blood through the blood vessels", "Food moving along the gullet to the stomach", "Muscles in the arm tightening and relaxing"], a: 1, why: "Each heartbeat pushes blood through the blood vessels, which is felt as a pulse. That is why pulse rate equals heart rate.", lvl: 2 },

    { q: "The bar graph shows Hui Min’s breathing rate during four activities, P, Q, R and S. Which shows the activities arranged from the least active to the most active?", fig: { type: "bar", xl: "Activity", yl: "Breathing rate (breaths per min)", bars: [["P", 18], ["Q", 45], ["R", 25], ["S", 14]] }, o: ["S, P, R, Q", "P, S, R, Q", "Q, R, P, S", "S, R, P, Q"], a: 0, why: "The more active she is, the more oxygen she needs and the faster she breathes: S (14) < P (18) < R (25) < Q (45).", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Which statement about gaseous exchange is NOT correct?", o: ["It takes place as air moves down the windpipe.", "It takes place in the lungs.", "Oxygen from the air in the lungs enters the blood.", "Carbon dioxide from the blood passes into the lungs."], a: 0, why: "The windpipe only carries air; gaseous exchange happens only in the lungs, where oxygen enters the blood and carbon dioxide leaves it.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Oxygen breathed in through the nose is used by the leg muscles. Study the diagram. Which shows the path the oxygen takes?", fig: { type: "organs", system: "circulatory", mark: { heart: "heart", lungs: "lungs" } }, o: ["Nose → lungs → windpipe → heart → leg muscles", "Nose → windpipe → heart → lungs → leg muscles", "Nose → windpipe → lungs → heart → leg muscles", "Windpipe → nose → lungs → heart → leg muscles"], a: 2, why: "Air goes nose → windpipe → lungs, where oxygen enters the blood. The blood goes to the heart, which pumps it through blood vessels to the leg muscles.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "A guppy was kept in tank A and another in tank B. The number of times each guppy’s gill covers moved in one minute was counted. Which statement best explains the results?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "A", icon: "box", lines: ["High dissolved oxygen", "Gill covers: 60/min"] }, { label: "B", icon: "box", lines: ["Low dissolved oxygen", "Gill covers: 110/min"] }] }, o: ["The water in B had less dissolved oxygen, so more water had to pass over its gills.", "The guppy in B was taking in air from above the water through its gills.", "The water in B had more carbon dioxide, which the guppy needed to take in.", "The guppy in B was using its lungs as well as its gills to breathe."], a: 0, why: "Gills take in oxygen dissolved in water. With less dissolved oxygen, more water must pass over the gills. Guppies have no lungs and gills cannot use air.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "The graph shows Ben’s breathing rate over 12 minutes. Which statement is correct?", fig: { type: "line", xl: "Time (min)", yl: "Breathing rate (breaths per min)", pts: [["0", 16], ["2", 16], ["4", 30], ["6", 38], ["8", 38], ["10", 26], ["12", 18]] }, o: ["His breathing rate went back to 16 breaths per min as soon as he stopped exercising.", "His breathing rate at 10 min was lower than at 2 min.", "He was resting from 4 min to 8 min.", "His breathing rate was highest from 6 min to 8 min."], a: 3, why: "The graph peaks at 38 breaths per min at 6 and 8 min. At 10 min (26) it is still above the resting rate of 16, so it did not return at once, and 26 is higher than 16.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Forty pupils sat in a small classroom with the doors and windows closed for two hours. Compared with the start, the air in the classroom most likely had ______.", o: ["more oxygen, less carbon dioxide and less water vapour", "less oxygen, more carbon dioxide and more water vapour", "less nitrogen, more oxygen and more water vapour", "the same amount of oxygen, carbon dioxide and water vapour"], a: 1, why: "The pupils took in oxygen and breathed out air with more carbon dioxide and water vapour. Nitrogen is not used, so it stays about the same.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "The table shows the heart rates of four pupils before and just after running 400 m. Whose heart rate increased the most?", tbl: [["Pupil", "Before (beats per min)", "After (beats per min)"], ["W", "70", "110"], ["X", "82", "118"], ["Y", "65", "112"], ["Z", "75", "105"]], o: ["W", "X", "Y", "Z"], a: 2, why: "Increases: W 40, X 36, Y 47, Z 30. X has the highest heart rate after running, but Y had the biggest increase.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "A pond was left with almost no dissolved oxygen in its water, but there was plenty of air above the surface. Which animal would be LEAST able to survive in the pond?", o: ["Dolphin", "Duck", "Goldfish", "Adult frog"], a: 2, why: "A goldfish can only take in oxygen dissolved in water through its gills. Dolphins, ducks and adult frogs have lungs and can take in air at the surface.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Which of these statements are correct? A: The circulatory system transports digested food absorbed in the small intestine. B: The respiratory system takes in oxygen, which enters the blood in the lungs. C: The heart digests food into simpler substances. D: Blood carries carbon dioxide from the body parts to the lungs.", o: ["A and B only", "A, B and D only", "B and D only", "A, B, C and D"], a: 1, why: "A, B and D describe the systems working together. C is wrong: the heart pumps blood; digestion takes place in the digestive system.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "In mouth-to-mouth rescue breathing, a rescuer breathes his exhaled air into a person who has stopped breathing. Why can this help the person?", o: ["Exhaled air contains no carbon dioxide.", "Exhaled air contains more nitrogen, which the person needs.", "Exhaled air contains more water vapour, which the lungs need to take in.", "Exhaled air still contains quite a lot of oxygen."], a: 3, why: "Exhaled air still has about 16% oxygen, enough to supply the person’s blood. It does contain carbon dioxide, and nitrogen is about the same and is not used.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "A girl breathed in and out of a sealed plastic bag 10 times. The table shows the results. Which conclusion is best supported?", tbl: [["Gas", "At the start (%)", "After 10 breaths (%)"], ["X", "78", "78"], ["Y", "21", "15"], ["Z", "0.04", "5"]], o: ["Y is oxygen taken into her blood; Z is carbon dioxide; X is nitrogen.", "X is oxygen, which her body does not need, so it stays the same.", "Z is oxygen, which her lungs gave out into the bag as she breathed.", "Y is nitrogen, which her body took in and used up for energy."], a: 0, why: "Y decreased (taken into the blood: oxygen), Z increased (removed from the blood: carbon dioxide), and X stayed the same (nitrogen, not used).", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Which statement is true of BOTH the gills of a fish and the lungs of a human?", o: ["Both take in oxygen from the air that the animal breathes in.", "Both are where oxygen enters the blood and carbon dioxide leaves it.", "Both take in oxygen that is dissolved in the water around them.", "Both pump blood around the body to carry oxygen to the cells."], a: 1, why: "Gills and lungs both carry out gaseous exchange with the blood. Gills use oxygen dissolved in water, lungs use air, and neither pumps blood.", lvl: 3 },

    { q: "Siti counted 18 pulse beats in 15 seconds while resting. Right after skipping, she counted 33 beats in 15 seconds. By how much did her heart rate increase, in beats per minute?", o: ["15", "45", "60", "132"], a: 2, why: "Resting: 18 × 4 = 72 per min. After: 33 × 4 = 132 per min. Increase = 132 − 72 = 60. 15 is only the 15-second difference; 132 is the rate, not the increase.", lvl: 4 },
    { q: "Two blood samples were taken: one from a blood vessel carrying blood TO the lungs, and one from a blood vessel carrying blood AWAY FROM the lungs. Which samples, in that order, are they most likely to be?", tbl: [["Sample", "Amount of oxygen", "Amount of carbon dioxide"], ["P", "Low", "High"], ["Q", "High", "Low"], ["R", "High", "High"], ["S", "Low", "Low"]], o: ["P, then Q", "Q, then P", "R, then S", "S, then R"], a: 0, why: "Blood going to the lungs comes from the body parts, so it has less oxygen and more carbon dioxide (P). In the lungs it gains oxygen and loses carbon dioxide, so blood leaving has more oxygen and less carbon dioxide (Q).", lvl: 4 },
    { q: "At the top of a very high mountain, each breath of air contains less oxygen than at sea level. A climber who has just arrived there finds that, even when resting, her breathing rate and heart rate are higher than usual. Which best explains this?", o: ["Her body needs less oxygen on the mountain, so the extra breaths remove more nitrogen.", "Faster breathing takes in enough oxygen, and a faster heart rate transports it around her body.", "Her lungs must work harder to remove the extra carbon dioxide found in mountain air.", "Her heart must pump more air into her lungs, so both her heart and breathing rates rise."], a: 1, why: "With less oxygen in each breath, she must breathe faster to take in the same amount of oxygen, and her heart pumps blood faster to transport it to her body. The heart pumps blood, not air.", lvl: 4 },
    { q: "Leaf L was placed in a beaker of hot water. The diagram shows what was seen at its lower surface (P) and upper surface (Q): many air bubbles came out of its lower surface and only a few came out of its upper surface. Which conclusion is best supported?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "P", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Leaf L in hot water", "Lower surface", "Many bubbles"] }, { label: "Q", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Leaf L in hot water", "Upper surface", "A few bubbles"] }] }, o: ["The upper surface of the leaf has no tiny openings at all.", "Air can enter and leave the leaf only through its lower surface.", "The leaf was taking in air from the hot water through its surfaces.", "The lower surface has more tiny openings than the upper surface."], a: 3, why: "The hot water makes air inside the leaf expand and escape through the tiny openings. More bubbles below means more openings below; a few bubbles above shows the upper surface has some openings, so ‘none’ and ‘only’ go too far.", lvl: 4 },
    { q: "Three identical sealed jars were kept in a dark cupboard for 2 days: J was empty, K had a live grasshopper and L had a small potted plant. The bar graph shows the oxygen in each jar after 2 days. Which conclusion is correct?", fig: { type: "bar", xl: "Jar", yl: "Oxygen after 2 days (%)", bars: [["J (empty)", 21], ["K (grasshopper)", 18], ["L (plant)", 20]] }, o: ["Both the grasshopper and the plant took in oxygen, and the grasshopper took in more.", "Only the grasshopper took in oxygen, because plants do not need oxygen.", "The plant gave out oxygen, so jar L had more oxygen than jar J.", "Jar J was not needed, as it had no living thing in it."], a: 0, why: "Compared with the control J (21%), K dropped 3% and L dropped 1%, so both living things took in oxygen, the grasshopper more. J is the control that shows the oxygen change is caused by the living things.", lvl: 4 },
    { q: "Which statements are part of the correct explanation of why breathing rate and heart rate increase during exercise? A: The muscles need more energy. B: More oxygen must be taken in. C: Oxygen and digested food must be transported to the muscles faster. D: More carbon dioxide must be removed. E: More nitrogen must be removed.", o: ["A and B only", "A, B and C only", "B, C and E only", "A, B, C and D only"], a: 3, why: "A, B, C and D form the full explanation. E is wrong: the body does not use or produce nitrogen, so there is no extra nitrogen to remove.", lvl: 4 }
  ],
  tf: [
    { s: "The nose, windpipe and lungs are parts of the respiratory system.", a: true, why: "These are the three organs of the respiratory system named in the syllabus." },
    { s: "Exhaled air contains only carbon dioxide.", a: false, why: "Exhaled air is a mixture: mostly nitrogen, about 16% oxygen, about 4% carbon dioxide, plus water vapour." },
    { s: "Gaseous exchange takes place in the windpipe.", a: false, why: "Gaseous exchange takes place only in the lungs; the windpipe just carries air." },
    { s: "Exhaled air contains more water vapour than inhaled air.", a: true, why: "Water vapour is given out in the air we breathe out – that is why breath fogs a cold mirror." },
    { s: "Exhaled air contains much less nitrogen than inhaled air.", a: false, why: "The body does not use nitrogen, so the amount stays about the same (about 78%)." },
    { s: "Fish take in oxygen that is dissolved in water through their gills.", a: true, why: "Water passes over the gills, which take in the dissolved oxygen." },
    { s: "Dolphins and whales use gills to take in oxygen.", a: false, why: "They are mammals with lungs and must come to the water surface to take in air." },
    { s: "Insects take in air through breathing holes on their bodies.", a: true, why: "The breathing holes (spiracles) lead to air tubes that carry air to the body." },
    { s: "Plants do not need oxygen.", a: false, why: "Plants are living things and need oxygen too; they take in air through tiny openings in their leaves." },
    { s: "Plants take in air through tiny openings in their leaves.", a: true, why: "Most of these tiny openings (pores) are on the lower surface of the leaf." },
    { s: "The heart makes the blood that it pumps.", a: false, why: "The heart’s job is to pump blood around the body; it does not make blood." },
    { s: "The heart pumps blood to all parts of the body through blood vessels.", a: true, why: "The heart pumps; the blood vessels carry blood to and from all parts of the body." },
    { s: "Blood only transports oxygen.", a: false, why: "Blood also transports digested food, water, carbon dioxide and other waste substances." },
    { s: "Blood carries carbon dioxide from the body parts to the lungs.", a: true, why: "Carbon dioxide passes from the blood into the lungs and is removed when we breathe out." },
    { s: "Our heart rate increases when we exercise.", a: true, why: "The heart beats faster so that more oxygen and digested food are transported to the muscles." },
    { s: "As soon as we stop exercising, our heart rate returns to its resting rate immediately.", a: false, why: "Heart rate falls gradually and takes a few minutes to return to the resting rate." },
    { s: "The pulse we feel at the wrist is caused by the heart pumping blood.", a: true, why: "Each heartbeat pushes blood through the blood vessels, so pulse rate equals heart rate." },
    { s: "The digestive system carries digested food to all parts of the body.", a: false, why: "Digested food is absorbed into the blood in the small intestine; the circulatory system carries it to body parts." },
    { s: "Blood leaving the lungs has more oxygen than blood entering the lungs.", a: true, why: "Oxygen from the air in the lungs enters the blood during gaseous exchange." },
    { s: "A person’s breathing rate is lower when running than when sleeping.", a: false, why: "Running needs more energy, so the body needs more oxygen and the breathing rate is higher." }
  ],
  sort: [
    { title: "Sort the living things by the part they use to take in air", groups: ["Lungs", "Gills", "Breathing holes", "Tiny openings in leaves"], items: [["cat", 0], ["dolphin", 0], ["chicken", 0], ["guppy", 1], ["shark", 1], ["tadpole", 1], ["grasshopper", 2], ["adult mosquito", 2], ["hibiscus plant", 3], ["fern", 3]] },
    { title: "Which kind of air does each statement describe?", groups: ["Inhaled air only", "Exhaled air only", "Both"], items: [["contains about 21% oxygen", 0], ["contains about 0.04% carbon dioxide", 0], ["contains about 16% oxygen", 1], ["contains about 4% carbon dioxide", 1], ["has more water vapour of the two", 1], ["turns limewater chalky faster", 1], ["is made up mostly of nitrogen", 2], ["contains some oxygen", 2], ["contains some carbon dioxide", 2]] },
    { title: "Sort the parts into their body systems", groups: ["Respiratory system", "Circulatory system", "Digestive system"], items: [["nose", 0], ["windpipe", 0], ["lungs", 0], ["heart", 1], ["blood vessels", 1], ["blood", 1], ["gullet", 2], ["stomach", 2], ["small intestine", 2], ["large intestine", 2]] }
  ],
  oe: [
    { q: "The diagram shows the human respiratory system. (a) Name part C. (b) State what happens to oxygen in part C. (c) State what happens to carbon dioxide in part C.", fig: { type: "organs", system: "respiratory", mark: { nose: "A", windpipe: "B", lungs: "C" } }, marks: 3, kw: [["lung"], ["enters the blood", "into the blood", "absorbed", "enter the blood", "goes into the blood", "taken in by the blood", "to the blood"], ["removed", "out of the blood", "leaves the blood", "breathed out", "breathe out", "from the blood"]], model: "(a) Lungs. (b) Oxygen from the air in the lungs enters the blood. (c) Carbon dioxide passes out of the blood into the lungs and is removed from the body when we breathe out." },
    { q: "The bar graph shows Mei’s breathing rate during three activities. (a) Describe how her breathing rate changed as the activity became more active. (b) Explain why her body needed this change. Give two reasons.", fig: { type: "bar", xl: "Activity", yl: "Breathing rate (breaths per min)", bars: [["Sitting", 15], ["Walking", 22], ["Running", 40]] }, marks: 3, kw: [["increase", "higher", "faster", "went up", "rose"], ["more oxygen"], ["carbon dioxide"]], model: "(a) Her breathing rate increased as the activity became more active. (b) Her muscles needed more energy, so she needed to take in more oxygen at a faster rate, and to remove more carbon dioxide at a faster rate." },
    { q: "The graph shows Jia Hui’s heart rate before, during and after skipping. (a) What was her heart rate at rest? (b) Explain why her heart rate increased when she was skipping.", fig: { type: "line", xl: "Time (min)", yl: "Heart rate (beats per min)", pts: [["0", 72], ["2", 72], ["4", 118], ["6", 128], ["8", 95], ["10", 76]] }, marks: 4, kw: [["72"], ["faster", "more quickly", "quicker"], ["oxygen"], ["digested food", "food", "nutrient"]], model: "(a) 72 beats per minute. (b) Her muscles needed more energy. Her heart beat faster to pump blood faster, so that more oxygen and digested food could be transported to her muscles more quickly." },
    { q: "(a) Name the part a fish uses to take in oxygen. (b) A fish is taken out of the water and left on the ground. Explain why it dies after some time, even though air contains oxygen.", marks: 3, kw: [["gill"], ["dissolved", "in water", "from water", "in the water", "from the water"], ["air", "enough oxygen"]], model: "(a) Gills. (b) Its gills can only take in oxygen dissolved in water. Out of the water, its gills cannot take in oxygen from the air, so it does not get enough oxygen and dies." },
    { q: "Oil was painted along both sides of a grasshopper’s body. The grasshopper died soon after. Explain why.", marks: 2, kw: [["breathing hole", "spiracle"], ["air", "oxygen"]], model: "The oil blocked the breathing holes along the sides of its body, so it could not take in air (oxygen) and died." },
    { q: "Leaf L was placed in a beaker of hot water. The diagram shows what was seen at its lower surface (P) and upper surface (Q): many air bubbles came out of its lower surface and only a few came out of its upper surface. (a) What do the bubbles show about the leaf? (b) What can you conclude about the lower surface compared with the upper surface? (c) Why does a plant need these openings?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "P", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Leaf L in hot water", "Lower surface", "Many bubbles"] }, { label: "Q", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Leaf L in hot water", "Upper surface", "A few bubbles"] }] }, marks: 3, kw: [["tiny opening", "opening", "pore", "hole"], ["more tiny openings", "more openings", "more pores", "more holes", "more of these"], ["take in air", "take in oxygen", "air can enter", "air to enter", "gases", "air enter", "take in gas"]], model: "(a) The leaf has tiny openings through which air can pass out of the leaf. (b) The lower surface has more tiny openings than the upper surface. (c) The openings let the plant take in air (so that it gets the oxygen it needs) and let gases enter and leave the leaf.", x: 1 },
    { q: "The table shows the percentage of three gases, X, Y and Z, in inhaled and exhaled air. (a) Identify gas Y. (b) Identify gas Z. (c) Explain why the amount of gas X is the same in inhaled and exhaled air.", tbl: [["Gas", "Inhaled air (%)", "Exhaled air (%)"], ["X", "78", "78"], ["Y", "21", "16"], ["Z", "0.04", "4"]], marks: 3, kw: [["oxygen"], ["carbon dioxide"], ["not use", "not used", "doesn't use", "don’t use", "doesn’t use", "don't use", "not taken", "not absorbed", "not need", "not needed"]], model: "(a) Y is oxygen. (b) Z is carbon dioxide. (c) X is nitrogen. The body does not use nitrogen, so the same amount is breathed out as is breathed in." },
    { q: "Limewater turns chalky when carbon dioxide passes through it. Ravi blew exhaled air into limewater A and pumped surrounding air into limewater B, as shown. The limewater in A turned chalky much faster. (a) Explain why. (b) State one thing Ravi must keep the same to make it a fair test.", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "A", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Limewater", "Exhaled air blown in"] }, { label: "B", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Limewater", "Surrounding air", "pumped in"] }] }, marks: 3, kw: [["more carbon dioxide"], ["removed", "from the blood", "gives out", "given out", "breathe out", "breathed out"], ["amount", "volume"]], model: "(a) Exhaled air contains more carbon dioxide than surrounding air, because carbon dioxide from the blood is removed in the lungs and breathed out. (b) The amount of limewater (or the volume of air passed through it) must be kept the same." },
    { q: "Explain how the three body systems below work together so that the leg muscles get what they need. (a) The digestive system (b) The respiratory system (c) The circulatory system", marks: 3, kw: [["absorb", "simpler substances"], ["oxygen"], ["transport", "carr"]], model: "(a) The digestive system breaks down food into simpler substances, which are absorbed into the blood in the small intestine. (b) The respiratory system takes in oxygen, which enters the blood in the lungs. (c) The heart pumps blood, which transports the digested food and oxygen through the blood vessels to the leg muscles.", x: 1 },
    { q: "The diagram shows the human circulatory system. (a) Name organ X. (b) Which gas does blood leaving the lungs have more of than blood entering the lungs? (c) Name one waste gas that blood carries from the body parts to the lungs.", fig: { type: "organs", system: "circulatory", mark: { heart: "X", lungs: "lungs" } }, marks: 3, kw: [["heart"], ["oxygen"], ["carbon dioxide"]], model: "(a) Heart. (b) Oxygen. (c) Carbon dioxide." },
    { q: "Ali counted his pulse for 15 seconds at rest and got 19 beats. (a) Calculate his heart rate in beats per minute. (b) After skipping for 5 minutes, his heart rate was much higher. Explain why his heart needed to beat faster.", marks: 3, kw: [["76"], ["faster", "more quickly", "quicker"], ["oxygen"]], model: "(a) 19 × 4 = 76 beats per minute. (b) His muscles needed more energy, so his heart pumped blood faster to transport more oxygen and digested food to his muscles more quickly." },
    { q: "Mosquito larvae live in water but hang just below the surface to take in air through a breathing tube. Explain why spreading a thin layer of oil on the water surface kills the larvae.", marks: 2, kw: [["block", "cover", "clog", "prevent", "stop"], ["air", "oxygen"]], model: "The oil covers the water surface and blocks the breathing tubes of the larvae, so they cannot take in air (oxygen) and they die.", x: 1 },
    { q: "Dolphins live in the sea but have to come up to the water surface regularly. Explain why.", marks: 2, kw: [["lung"], ["air"]], model: "Dolphins take in oxygen through lungs, not gills, so they must come up to the surface to take in air." },
    { q: "A live grasshopper was placed in a sealed jar. The graph shows the amount of oxygen in the jar over 4 days. (a) Describe how the amount of oxygen changed. (b) Explain this change. (c) Predict how the amount of carbon dioxide in the jar changed.", fig: { type: "line", xl: "Day", yl: "Oxygen in the jar (%)", pts: [["0", 21], ["1", 20], ["2", 19], ["3", 18], ["4", 17]] }, marks: 3, kw: [["decrease", "less", "fell", "drop", "went down", "lower"], ["took in", "take in", "used", "uses", "breathe"], ["increase", "more carbon dioxide", "went up", "rose", "higher"]], model: "(a) The amount of oxygen decreased steadily. (b) The grasshopper took in oxygen from the air in the jar through its breathing holes. (c) The amount of carbon dioxide increased, because the grasshopper gave out carbon dioxide." }
  ],
  doc: [
    { q: "Explain why Mei breathes faster when she runs.", marks: 2, answers: ["Because she is tired from running.", "Her muscles need more energy, so her body must take in more oxygen and remove more carbon dioxide at a faster rate.", "So that she can breathe out more oxygen."], best: 1, why: "Answer 2 gives both ideas: more oxygen taken in and more carbon dioxide removed. Answer 1 gives a feeling, not a scientific reason. Answer 3 is wrong – she needs to take IN more oxygen, not breathe it out." },
    { q: "Explain why exhaled air turns limewater chalky faster than surrounding air.", marks: 2, answers: ["Exhaled air contains more carbon dioxide than surrounding air, because carbon dioxide from the blood is removed in the lungs and breathed out.", "Exhaled air is carbon dioxide.", "Because exhaled air is warmer."], best: 0, why: "Answer 1 compares the amount of carbon dioxide and explains where it comes from. Answer 2 is wrong – exhaled air is a mixture that is mostly nitrogen. Answer 3 gives a reason that does not affect limewater." },
    { q: "Oil was sprayed on the sides of a grasshopper’s body and it died. Explain why.", marks: 2, answers: ["It could not breathe.", "The oil was poisonous.", "The oil blocked its breathing holes, so it could not take in air."], best: 2, why: "Answer 3 names the breathing holes and says air could not be taken in. Answer 1 is too vague – it does not name the part. Answer 2 guesses a reason that the question gives no evidence for." },
    { q: "Explain why the heart beats faster during exercise.", marks: 2, answers: ["Because the heart needs more oxygen.", "The heart pumps blood faster so that more oxygen and digested food are transported to the muscles more quickly.", "Because the person is excited."], best: 1, why: "Answer 2 links faster pumping to transporting more oxygen and digested food to the muscles. Answer 1 misses the point – the extra blood is pumped to supply the working muscles, and it does not say what the blood transports. Answer 3 is not a scientific reason." },
    { q: "Explain why a fish dies when it is kept out of water for a long time.", marks: 2, answers: ["Its gills can only take in oxygen dissolved in water, so out of water it cannot take in enough oxygen.", "It cannot breathe because it has no nose.", "Fish need water to drink."], best: 0, why: "Answer 1 names the gills and the key idea of dissolved oxygen. Answer 2 names the wrong part – fish use gills, not a nose. Answer 3 gives a wrong reason; the problem is oxygen, not drinking." }
  ]
};
